  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CONSERVING KEY SPECIES AND HABITATS OF THE CHIQUIBUL-MAYA MOUNTAIN LANDSCAPE IN BELIZE</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to support a conservation effort in Belizes Chiquibul-Maya Mountains by strengthening law enforcement efforts and promoting indigenous stewardship of forests in Maya communal lands. The project is intended to conserve one of Central Americas largest intact blocks of tropical forest, which harbors emblematic species such as jaguars, scarlet macaws, and tapirs, as well as hundreds of protected plant and animal species, by addressing poaching and trafficking of scarlet macaws and habitat loss. Activities include: (1) aerial and ground reconnaissance to document cattle ranching along a 90 km extension of the Maya Mountain Massif located along the Belize-Guatemala border; (2) biological monitoring of nesting areas and mobilization of an Anti-Poaching Unit to reduce poaching and trade of scarlet macaws; and (3) a public outreach campaign, documentation of indigenous practices, and demonstration projects that support indigenous forest stewardship in five communities of the eastern Maya Mountains.</narrative>
  </description>
